---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const unempCfg = {
  params: [
    { name: 'lf', label: 'labour force (millions)', min: 50, max: 200, step: 5, value: 100 },
    { name: 'unemployed', label: 'unemployed (millions)', min: 2, max: 40, step: 1, value: 8 },
  ],
  readouts: [
    { label: 'unemployment rate', expr: 'unemployed/lf*100', unit: '%' },
    { label: 'employed', expr: 'lf - unemployed', unit: 'M' },
    { label: 'Okun\'s estimate: GDP gap vs full employment', expr: '2*(unemployed/lf*100 - 4)', unit: '%' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/economics/intermediate/unemployment-labour-markets">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Unemployment rate = unemployed ÷ labour force — where the labour force is those
    <strong>working or actively seeking work</strong> (the discouraged and retired don't count,
    which is why rates can fall when people give up). Economists separate three kinds:
    <strong>frictional</strong> (between jobs — healthy), <strong>structural</strong> (skills
    mismatched to available jobs — painful), and <strong>cyclical</strong> (recessions — the part
    policy targets).
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the rate, its arithmetic, and Okun's rough law linking jobs to output" widget="sliderLab" config={unempCfg} />

  <p>
    Okun's rule of thumb: each percentage point of unemployment above ~4% costs roughly 2% of GDP
    versus potential. The 4% figure is the "natural rate" — the frictional floor an economy
    never goes below without overheating.
  </p>

  <h2>Why unemployment exists at all</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Wages are sticky downward:</strong> workers and firms resist pay cuts, so recessions shed jobs instead of wages. Keynes made stickiness central; economists still debate its exact causes (contracts, morale, fairness norms).</li>
    <li><strong>Search takes time:</strong> matching workers to vacancies is a process (the search-and-matching model won the 2010 Nobel). Unemployment insurance lengthens searches but also improves their quality — the trade-off every benefits debate re-litigates.</li>
    <li><strong>Structural change hurts locally:</strong> automation and trade shift jobs between sectors faster than workers retrain. The aggregate unemployment rate hides the concentrated pain — "China shock" research found affected regions stayed depressed for a decade or more.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Okun (1962); Diamond–Mortensen–Pissarides framework (Nobel 2010); Autor, Dorn & Hanson, AER (2013).</p>
</TopicPage>
